1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
A dog's nose splits air into two jobs.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:09,062
When a dog sniffs, air does not take
one simple path through the nose.

3
00:00:09,062 --> 00:00:10,000
It splits.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,000
A 2010 study built a computer model of
a dog's nasal airway from MRI scans.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The simulation showed two
separate flows: one for breathing,

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
and one that carries odor
molecules to the olfactory region.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,350
Each nostril also draws from
its own patch of air, because

8
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:35,000
the nose's reach is smaller than
the gap between the nostrils.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,784
That may help a dog compare
scent strength on each side.

10
00:00:38,784 --> 00:00:42,162
The nose is a routing
system, not just a detector.

11
00:00:42,162 --> 00:00:45,000
Its shape helps scent reach the receptors.
